t right yeah quote unquote  call stack  so this shows you can do this with any  recursive algorithm by the way  everything you can do with recursion you  can do with a loop and a stack there is  nothing magical about recursion at all  but please don't do this ever  okay so  you know if if recursion isn't more  powerful than iteration and recursion is  often uh harder to understand than recur  than iteration  why should we ever use recursion so i  thought about this a lot and i came up  with an answer and then i thought about  some more and i came up with a better  answer  recursion maps very neatly to problems  that have a tree-like structure and also  require backtracking you really need to  have both of these features for a  recursive algorithm to be very very  useful and and elegant  um fortunately there's a lot of stuff in  computer science that involve trees and  require backtracking a tree is really  just a node that branches off to two  other nodes and then these nodes branch  off to other nodes so it's naturally  recursive and if you think about it a  list is really just a tree that has one  branch on each node so list can also use  recursion as well  a maze has recursive solutions because  really a maze is just a giant tree that  you can from each node take one of  several paths from which take you to  another node and then you can choose one  of several paths from that every once in  a while you'll reach a dead end so  you'll have to backtrack  to an earlier node this is using a lot  of path finding and ai algorithms as  well  your file system on your hard drive is a  tree you have folders that contain other  folders that's that's recursion  so um if you don't have a  a need for backtracking in a tree-like  structure you really don't have to use  recursion when i showed you that uh that  example where you can turn any recursive  algorithm into a loop and a stack  technically you can do the opposite as  well you can turn all loops into a  recursive function