u're making a  tail call and then it does all the magic  with the call stack behind the scenes  but the thing is the c python  interpreter does not implement  tail call optimization  and the other thing is it never will  so  the reason  other than that you have to it  encourages you to bangle your code but  uh there's another reason for it well  the short answer is it's simply  unpythonic  the longer answer uh is explained in a  few blog posts by guido van rossum so  you can't even use tail call  optimization in python it's totally  irrelevant and there's a there's a  second problem that we always use when  we're explaining recursion to beginners  and it's also a terrible candidate for  recursion and that is the fibonacci  sequence  oh yes it's great so if you don't know  about this it's a sequence of numbers  the first two digits are one and one and  the next digit is just the sum of the  two previous digits so one plus one is  two one plus two is three two plus three  is five and so on and so on forever um  you can write a function that says okay  find the nth fibonacci number and this  is going to be a recursive function or  it can be a recursive function because  fibonacci has a recursive nature  the nth fibonacci number is just the sum  of the nth minus 1 and nth minus 2  fibonacci numbers and the base case is  that the first and second fibonacci  numbers are both one so we can write a  function  like this  and it works for really small numbers  if you try to  calculate the 35th fibonacci number  that'll surprisingly take several  seconds and if you try to calculate the  100th fibonacci numbers that will take  several centuries and the reason is  because each call to fibonacci  generates two more calls to fibonacci so  it's like a hydra every head you cut off  two more grow in its place and this  means we're doing a lot of work an  exponentially growing amount of work and  worse yet  a lot of that work is redundant like  we're making multiple calls to fibonacci  4 or fibonacci 3 right here  so the trick around this  is to cache all the return values so  that you know the first time you  calculate fibonacci of 4 you remember  that so in future calls with fibonacci  of iv you can just return that cached  value instead and that prunes away this  tree and and removes all the redundant  work but again that's sort of a trick  you have to do  just so you can solve a problem that  iteration doesn't have to begin with  now this technique is called memoization  which your phone will always want to  auto correct to memorization but it's  memoization  um we can just you know create a cache  and then at the start of the function  check the argument return the cache  value if it's in there otherwise we'll  just later update the cache but python  is the battery's included language so  you can just  use the funk tools.lru cache decorator  and it does the exact same thing you can  do this with any function it's also good  outside of recursion but it's especially  useful with recursion  lru stands for least recently used it's  a very common caching algorithm  but again that's just to fix something  that iteration doesn't have a problem  with anyway  uh so i hope i've gone and explained how  recursion is is not this magical you  code sorcery thing that only the  mightiest of programmers can wield  in fact sometimes it's a completely  inappropriate way to solve your  programming problems  yeah so let's recap there's so much more  to recursion but i hope i've just  started you off with a lot of really  nice basics  the more you practice coming up with  recursive solutions the better you get  at sort of identifying the the general  gist of how recursion works but to recap  to understand recursion you must first  understand stacks and the call stack  this is the main reason why recursion is  so confusing to beginners is because the  call stack doesn't appear in your source  in your source code so it's easy to  forget about it and easy to forget that  it's there  you'll always need one base case and one  recursive case in your recursive  function and function calls push a frame  object onto the call stack and returning  from a function pops them off of the  call stack  and a stack overflow happens when you  never reach your base case and you just  keep making recursive function calls  over and over and over again without  returning  anything you can do with recursion you  can do with a loop and a stack there's  nothing magical about recursion  and using recursion only uh when you  have a problem that has a tree-like  structure and requires backtracking i  think backtracking is the main key there  otherwise it's not completely necessary  uh tail call elimination and memoization  are two tricks that help make recursion  practical to run on real world computers  uh tail call elimination is com or yeah  tail call optimization is completely  irrelevant to python because it doesn't  implement it in the interpreter  and that's it that is everything i've  covered i'm really glad that i was able  to get through all of this in 30 minutes  here's where you can download the slides  and you can reach me on twitter and at  email thank you so much and enjoy the  rest of the conference
 

